    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Most people meet dry eye as a lubrication problem. The melanocortin angle is that a large
    part of it is also an <strong>inflammatory</strong> problem of the ocular surface — and the
    eye already keeps itself quiet with the same peptides this site maps everywhere else.
    That is why a melanocortin eye drop is not a stretch: it is asking a tissue to do more of
    what it already knows how to do.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>An inflamed surface, not just a dry one</h2>
  <p>
    Dry eye disease sits at the intersection of tear-film instability, neurosensory
    symptoms, and chronic low-grade inflammation of the cornea and conjunctiva. Patients feel
    burning, grittiness, fluctuating vision and pain; clinicians measure staining, tear
    break-up time and other signs that do not always move in lockstep with how the eye feels.
    That signs-versus-symptoms split is exactly why dry-eye trials are hard — and why a drug
    that only lubricates can leave the inflammatory driver untouched.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>Why the eye already speaks melanocortin</h2>
  <p>
    Inside the eye, <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> is part of the quiet that maintains ocular
    immune privilege. On the surface itself, α-MSH protects corneal epithelial cells in a rat
    model of dry eye and in human corneal epithelium, and people with dry eye show altered
    levels of α-MSH and related neuropeptides that track with clinical measures.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} />
    The broader ocular melanocortin story also includes uveitis models in which agonists
    suppress autoimmune inflammation — evidence that several melanocortin receptors, not just
    one, sit on the immune cells of the eye.<Cite n={7} />
  </p>
  <p>
    So the mechanism bet is straightforward: activate melanocortin receptors on the ocular
    surface to dampen inflammatory cytokines and nudge the tissue toward resolution, rather
    than flooding it with lubricant or broadly suppressing immunity. That is the same
    anti-inflammatory arm mapped on the <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page, aimed
    at a tissue that already uses it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the pipeline is doing</h2>
  <p>
    The clinical vehicle for this idea is <a href="/pl-9643">PL-9643</a> — an investigational
    melanocortin pan-agonist eye drop (active at every melanocortin receptor except adrenal
    MC2R) originally developed by Palatin. In Phase 2 it did not beat vehicle on primary
    endpoints in the whole population, but improved several signs in the moderate-to-severe
    subgroup and set up a pivotal programme.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The pivotal <strong>MELODY-1</strong> trial enrolled 575 patients with moderate-to-severe
    dry eye. It met its co-primary <em>symptom</em> endpoint of ocular pain — with improvement
    emerging by week 2 and a favourable safety profile — but missed its co-primary{' '}
    <em>sign</em> endpoint of conjunctival staining.<Cite n={4} /> Two further trials,{' '}
    <strong>MELODY-2</strong> and <strong>MELODY-3</strong>, are designed to evaluate both
    signs and symptoms; the FDA has accepted that design, and in early 2026 Palatin sublicensed
    the programme to Altanispac Labs, which now owns the remaining Phase 3 work subject to
    funding.<Cite n={5} /><Cite n={6} />
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Dry eye is a natural melanocortin indication because the eye already runs on this quieting
    biology — but a positive symptom readout is not an approval, and the remaining trials still
    have to show both what patients feel and what clinicians measure. For the drug itself see{' '}
    <a href="/pl-9643">PL-9643</a>; for the sister gut programme that uses the same resolution
    idea in a different organ, see <a href="/ulcerative-colitis">ulcerative colitis</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a>; for the dated snapshot across the field, see the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>.
  </p>

